Abstract. Organic-inorganic heterojunction perovskite solar cell (PSC) is promising for low-cost and high-performance photovoltaics. To further promote the performance of PSCs, understanding and controlling the underneath photoconversion mechanisms are highly necessary. Here, we present a comprehensive opto-electro-thermal (OET) study on the heterojunction PSCs by quantitatively addressing the coupled optical, carrier transport and thermodynamic behaviors within the device. With achieving a well agreement with the experiment, we theoretically explore the thermodynamic mechanisms involving the energy conversions and focus especially on the origins of the various energy losses in PSCs. We summarize six categories of microscopic heat conversion processes in the heterojunction PSC, where the Joule and Peltier heats can be defined as the intrinsic losses in PSCs. Moreover, we also discuss the possible manipulation methods to decrease the energy losses, e.g., by tailoring the doping concentration and energy-level alignment. An exemplified OET optimization is also presented, which predicts that the PCE of the fabricated PSC can be enhanced from $21.37\%$ to $23.84\%$ .
Keywords: photovoltaics; perovskite solar cells; opto-electro-thermal physics; multiphysics simulation;   
heterojunction devices; optimization design.
Heterojunction photovoltaics (PV) have attracted tremendous interests due to their potentials in obtaining high optoelectronic performance through the advanced hetero-interfaces design. The typical high-performance PV systems include for example the HIT (heterojunction with intrinsic thin-layer) silicon or GaAs solar cells (SCs) as well as the heterojunction perovskite solar cells (PSCs).13 Especially, in the last decade, the certified power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of single-junction PSCs have reached $\sim25.2\%$ (up to $\sim28\%$ for tandem configuration).3 This inspiring progress can be attributed to the desirable optoelectronic properties of perovskite materials and the superior device configuration. First, perovskite light-absorbers have unique superiorities, including high absorption coefficient, long carrier diffusion length and low exciton binding energy;46 second, the intrinsic (or lightly doped) perovskite layer is sandwiched between the hole-transporting layer (HTL) and electron-transporting layer (ETL) so that a heterojunction pair is formed, which ensures an effective separation and transportation of the photogenerated carriers.79 Despite the tremendous progresses, challenges still exist widely which prevent from further increasing the PCEs of PSC systems. Particularly, there are many important thermal effects in SCs, e.g., thermalization, Joule, and Peltier heats,10, 11 which raise the device temperature as well as significantly degrade the performance and stability of the device.1215 Actually, the energy losses of SCs can be caused by the coupled factors in optical, electrical, and thermal domains; hence, the precise inspection of the carrier dynamical characteristics in PSCs is highly useful.10,11 However, an accurate guidance to the design of high-performance PSCs is still rare since it is difficult to quantitatively estimate these fundamental mechanisms in PSCs. Such a work requires precisely addressing the detailed microscopic processes related to the heat generation and conversion in heterojunction SCs so that the advanced manipulation strategies for higher PCEs can be reached.
Recent efforts focus particularly on the fundamental physics and novel approaches to improve the performance of PSCs, including tuning the optoelectronic property of materials, engineering the heterojunction interfaces, and managing the incident photons. For example, the introduction of small amount of inorganic cations or additive agents into the perovskite precursors could simultaneously improve the efficiency and stability of PSCs.1620 Via the interface engineering, researchers have investigated the carrier recombination dynamic and hysteresis effects,2125 which demonstrated that the favorable heterojunction interface could decrease the open-circuit voltage ( $\mathrm{(\it{V_{\mathrm{oc}}})}$ loss and suppress the hysteresis effect.23,25 Also, the nanostructured PSCs could simultaneously enhance the light-harvesting and carrier separation characteristics at patterned heterojunction interface.2629 Theoretically, growing efforts have been made on studying the fundamental physics of PSCs through for example the firstprinciple calculation or the optoelectronic simulation.30,31 Previously, we performed the optoelectronic simulation of planar PSCs and discussed the controlling of the carrier transportation dynamics through self-doping effect.30 Xuan et al. quantified energy losses based on one-dimensional diffusion-drift method in planar and mesoporous PSCs, as well as, presented possible optimization strategies to promote the device performance.32,33
However, the microscopic energy conversion mechanisms in heterojunction photovoltaic devices as well as a detailed understanding on the energy losses from opto-electro-thermal (OET) perspectives are still unclear. This requires an OET simulation with carefully addressing the coupled multi-domain responses for the heterojunction photovoltaic devices. In our previous work, we reported our progress in realizing OET simulation for GaAs SCs,10 where the homojunction system was discussed without surmounting the challenges in OET simulation of heterojunction systems. Actually, for PSCs, even the advanced optoelectronic simulations are still rare.30 Therefore, an advanced OET simulation with addressing the profound underlying optical, electrical, and thermodynamic physics together with the comparison with experiments is of a high necessity for the development of PSCs.
In this paper, we report the realization of the OET simulation of heterojunction PSCs. With analyzing the underlying OET physics, the coupled OET physics and the simulation methodology on treating especially the heat components are presented. The fabrication and material/performance characterization of a planar PSC are then performed, which exhibits a good agreement with the simulation. We then quantitatively examine the energy losses of the fabricated PSC with considering the coupled optical, electrical and thermal factors. With distinguishingly discuss the origins and effects of the energy losses, we investigate the possible manipulation strategies of these microscopic heat mechanisms, e.g., by optimally designing the doping concentration and energy level alignment. Finally, we present an OET optimization to the considered heterojunction PSC by introducing the photon management, carrier transport optimization, and heat-generation suppression. It shows that the PCE of the PSC can be increased from $21.37\%$ to $23.84\%$ . Such a multiphysics OET study combing the advanced simulation and the experiment is beneficial for exploring the fundamental semiconductor-device sciences as well as promoting the advances of photovoltaic and photoconversion devices.
The OET Physics and Theories of Heterojunction SCs. For PSCs, an optoelectronic simulation can be realized by considering the electromagnetic and carrier electrodynamic responses.30 However, the further OET simulation has to mimic the rigorous thermodynamic responses within the device, namely, it must address the coupled optical, electrical and thermal responses simultaneously. Optically, the Maxwell’s equations are solved to obtain the detailed electromagnetic field distributions within the device so that the important optical parameters including the reflection, absorption and transmission can be achieved. Electrically, the coupled electron/hole transportation equations and Poisson’s equation for electrostatic potential are solved self-consistently. Thus, the carrier electrodynamics (e.g., carrier generation, recombination, transportation, and collection) can be estimated under the driving force generated by the carrier concentration gradient, built-in electric field, temperature gradient, material differences of the heterojunction, etc.10,30,34,35 Thermodynamically, the carrier thermodynamic and heat diffusion equations are solved together to estimate the carrier heat absorption and relaxation under the energy conservation principle. Here, special attention has to be paid on the heterojunction system, which requires taking account of the specific semiconductor parameters of the constituting materials in the transportation equations. Moreover, two typical assumptions are used in this study, i.e., 1) the carrier dynamic characteristics (e.g., generation, recombination, transportation) are considered under the thermal equilibrium situation and 2) an incident photon is absorbed to generate an electron-hole pair rather than the exciton. In addition, the challenge of long-time stability and hysteresis effect obtained in PSCs can now be effectively controlled, e.g., by material synthesis, constructing the stable heterojunction and encapsulation methods;14,15,22,36,37 therefore, here we mainly focus on exploring the underlying OET physics and manipulation technology instead of the material stability. The OET model can be expressed as follows:10
$$
\nabla\times(\nabla\times\boldsymbol{E})=k_{0}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{c}}\boldsymbol{E}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{G(x,y,z)=\int g(x,y,z,\lambda)\mathrm{d}\lambda}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\nabla\cdot\bigg[-D_{\mathfrak{n}}\nabla n+n\mu_{\mathfrak{n}}\Big(\nabla\phi+\frac{\nabla\chi}{q}+\frac{K_{\mathtt{b}}T}{q}\nabla\mathrm{ln}N_{\mathrm{c}}\Big)-n D_{\mathrm{th}}^{\mathfrak{n}}\nabla T\bigg]=G(x,y,z)-U}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\nabla\cdot\Big[-D_{\mathrm{p}}\nabla p-p\mu_{\mathrm{p}}\Big(\nabla\phi+\frac{\nabla\chi}{q}+\frac{\nabla E_{\mathrm{g}}}{q}-\frac{K_{\mathrm{B}}T}{q}\nabla\mathrm{ln}N_{\mathrm{v}}\Big)-p D\mathbb{f}_{\mathrm{h}}\nabla T\Big]=G(x,y,z)-U}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\nabla^{2}\phi=\frac{q}{\varepsilon_{0}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}(n-p-C)}\end{array}
$$
$$
\nabla\cdot\left(-\kappa\nabla T\right)=H
$$
where $E$ is the electromagnetic field, $k_{0}$ the wave vector in free space, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{c}}$ the frequency-dependent complex permittivity, $g(x,y,z,\lambda)$ the photogeneration rate of the carriers at a specific wavelength $\lambda$ in position $(x,y,z),n\left(p\right)$ the electron (hole) concentration, $D_{\mathfrak{n}}\left(D_{\mathfrak{p}}\right)$ the electron (hole) diffusion coefficient,
$D_{\mathrm{\th}}^{\mathrm{n}}\left(D_{\mathrm{\th}}^{\mathrm{p}}\right)$ the thermal diffusion coefficient of the electron (hole), $N_{\mathrm{c}}\left(N_{\mathrm{v}}\right)$ the conduction (valence) band density of states, $\mu_{\mathrm{n}}\left(\mu_{\mathrm{p}}\right)$ the electron (hole) mobility, $U$ the total bulk recombination, $\phi$ the electrostatic potential, $q$ the electron charge, $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{0}}$ $\left(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}\right)$ the vacuum (static) relative permittivity, $C$ the impurity concentration, $\kappa$ the thermal conductivity, $T$ the space-dependent temperature within the device, and $H$ the heat sources in the SC under steady state.
The detailed balance analysis reveals that the theoretical limit of PCE is $\sim33\ \%$ for sing-junction SCs under standard $A M1.5$ illumination, implying that a significant portion of the absorbed photons are converted into the unwanted heats that significantly raise the cell temperature. In order to effectively manage the electricity collection by controlling the heat generations in heterojunction photovoltaic devices, the fundamental carrier thermodynamic mechanisms have to be investigated. As shown in Figure 1, the photogenerated carriers undergo six energy relaxation stages before being collected, where the happened microscopic processes include: (a) thermalization heat $(H_{\mathrm{th}})$ , (b) Joule heat $(H_{\mathrm{Joule}})$ , (c) Peltier heat of semiconductor-heterojunction interface $(H_{P e l t i e r}^{S S})$ , (d) bulk recombination heat $(H_{\mathrm{rec}})$ , (e) Peltier heat of semiconductor-metal interface $(H_{P e l t i e r}^{S M})$ , and (f) surface recombination heat $(H_{\mathrm{surf}})$ . Many of these heat generation processes have been discussed in our previous paper on OET-simulation of homojunction SC system.10 However, for the heterojunction devices, the $H_{P e l t i e r}^{S S}$ is a special heat loss caused by the energy level difference of heterojunction interface (e.g., conduction/valence-band offsets). Generally, when the carriers drift or diffuse over the heterojunction interface, they will compensate for the energy difference of materials by exchanging heat with the surrounding lattice.38 The related Peltier heats can be defined as:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{H_{\mathrm{Peltier\_SS}}^{\mathrm{n}}=\Delta E_{\mathrm{c}}\cdot J_{\mathrm{n}}}\\ {\phantom{\frac{1}{2}}H\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{eltier\_SS}}=\Delta E_{\mathrm{v}}\cdot J_{\mathrm{p}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\Delta E_{\mathrm{c}}\left(\Delta E_{\mathrm{v}}\right)$ is the offset of conduction (valance) band between the two materials. There have the relationships: $\Delta E_{\mathrm{c}}=\Delta\chi$ and $\Delta E_{\mathrm{v}}=\Delta\chi+\Delta E_{\mathrm{g}}$ , where $\Delta\chi$ and $\Delta E_{\mathrm{g}}$ are the differences of affinity and bandgap of the materials, respectively. For the heterojunction PSC, the positive energy difference of perovskite and ETL or HTL interface can be defined as: $(\Delta\mathrm{E_{c}}>0,\chi_{\mathrm{ETL}}>\chi_{\mathrm{Per}})$ or $[\Delta\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{v}}>0,(\chi_{\mathrm{per}}+\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g\_Per}})>(\chi_{\mathrm{HTL}}+$ $\mathrm{E_{g\_HTL}})\dot{}_{-}$ ]. Hence, the heat absorption or cooling effect can all be observed relying on the direction of carrier movement.39, 40
## Results and Discussion
We fabricated a planar PSC with $\mathrm{MA}_{0.7}\mathrm{FA}_{0.3}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ (MA: methylammonium, FA: formamidinium) absorber under the regular (n-i-p) configuration, as shown in Figure 2(a). The detailed fabrication method can refer to the experimental section. A cross-sectional scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of a representatively complete PSC is shown in Figure 2(b), where the respective layers from bottom to top are as following: FTO ( $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ :F, $320~\mathrm{nm}$ , cathode)/ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ , ETL)/ $\mathrm{MA}_{0.7}\mathrm{FA}_{0.3}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ ( $620~\mathrm{nm}$ , perovskite) /Spiro-OMeTAD ( $150~\mathrm{nm}$ , HTL)/ Au ( $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ , anode).40 The prepared $\mathrm{MA_{0.7}F A_{0.3}P b I_{3}}$ film has a typical bandgap of $\sim1.57\mathrm{eV}$ ,41,42 which is consistent with the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectrum with the peak wavelength at $790~\mathrm{nm}$ (see Figure S1, Supporting information). The Xphotoelectron spectroscopy (XRD) spectrum of the $\mathrm{MA_{0.7}F A_{0.3}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite film is shown in Figure S2 (Supporting information), which exhibits two distinct peaks corresponding to the (110) and (220) crystal planes of the tetragonal phase. To estimate the optoelectronic property of the perovskite layer, Figure 2(c) shows the transient photoluminescence spectroscopy (TRPL) of the perovskite film deposited on a bare glass. The PL decay is fitted by the bi-exponential function: $\mathrm{I(t)~=~A_{1}e x p\left(-t/\tau_{1}\right)~+~A_{2}}$ exp $(-\mathrm{~t~}/\mathrm{~\tau~}_{2})$ , where $\boldsymbol{A}_{1}$ and $A_{2}$ are the refactors, $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ are the short and long quenching lifetimes, respectively.43 The fitted PL decay result proves that mean lifetime of the photogenerated carriers in the obtained perovskite film is $\sim2175$ ns [see Figure 2(c)], corresponding to an ultralow density of trap states. The SEM image also shows that the morphology of the $\mathrm{MA}_{0.7}\mathrm{FA}_{0.3}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ perovskite film is highly homogenous and dense with large crystalline domains (Figure S3, Supporting information). The highquality perovskite film guarantees a high PCE, as shown in Figure 2(d), i.e., the short-circuit current density $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=23.05~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , open-circuit voltage $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=1.135\:\mathrm{V}$ , and fill factor $F F=80.4\%$ , yielding a $\mathrm{PCE}=21.02\%$ . We performed the corresponding optoelectronic simulation as shown in Figure 2(d), with using the key simulation parameters from existing experimental literatures (see Table S1, Supporting information).12,30 It can be seen that the simulation reaches a very good agreement with the experiment.
Reaching the agreement between simulation and experiment, we now turn to the analysis of the PSCs from OET perspectives. To effectively guide the design of high-performance PSCs, the first issue should be a quantitative analysis of the energy losses with addressing the optical, electrical, thermal and temperature factors. Figure 3(a) plots the optical characteristics of the PSC with distinguishing the device overall reflection and the absorptions by different layers (i.e., FTO, Au, perovskite, ETL, and HTL). Under illumination, the incident sunlight first interacts with the FTO electrode, which thus shows a high parasitic absorption in the short-wavelength range (e.g., $300\leq\lambda\leq400\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). At the range of $400\leq\lambda\leq$
$790\ \mathrm{nm},\sim10\%$ of the sunlight is found to be reflected due to the optical impedance mismatch between the FTO glass and air. The light over the absorption cut-off wavelength of perovskite material (e.g., $790-1100\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) is strongly reflected back by the Au electrode. The ETL and HTL materials do not noticeably absorb the incident light in the operating band of PSCs as expected. Based on the analysis, the optical losses of the discussed PSC are primarily caused by the light reflection and the parasitic absorption of FTO. Therefore, an advanced design incorporating simultaneously the FTO thickness reduction and photon management could be an effective way to reduce the optical losses of PSCs.2528
Besides the optical losses, there always exist various kinds of channels which lead the absorbed photon energies to be converted into heats rather than the electricity. Therefore, the microscopic energy conversion processes from photons to free carriers or phonons have to be uncovered, which relies on a further insight into the carrier electrodynamic and thermodynamic behaviors in PSCs. Shown in Figure 3(b) is the pie chart of the energy components converted into electricity or consumed by various processes at the SC operation point, i.e., with the maximum output power density $P_{\mathrm{PV}}$ . Here, the $P_{\mathrm{th}}$ denotes the energy loss by thermalization,10 $P_{\mathrm{{Joule}}}$ by the Joule heat,10 $P_{P e l t i e r}^{S S}$ by the Peltier heat at heterojunction interface, $P_{P e l t i e r}^{S M}$ by the Peltier heat at semiconductor-metal interface,10 and $P_{\mathrm{U}}$ by the total recombination heat.10 Our OET simulation verifies that the sum of all electricity and heat components is exactly identical to the absorbed sunlight power density, i.e., strictly obeying the conservation of energy:
$$
P_{s u n}=P_{t h}+P_{J o u l e}+P_{P e l t i e r}^{S S}+P_{P e l t i e r}^{S M}+P_{U}+P_{P V}
$$
For more detailed information, the energy densities consumed by various heat mechanisms in PSC are listed in Table 1. Based on the detailed calculation, Figure 3(b) marks the percentages of all above energy components to be $46\%$ , $26.5\%$ , $16.1\%$ , $7.4\%$ , $4.6\%$ and $3.4\%$ , respectively. This means that, although a reasonable PCE $\sim21\%$ is achieved, only $\sim46\%$ of the absorption has been successfully converted into the electricity, leaving almost $54\%$ as the heat. Obviously, a fundamental strategy to enhance the electrical output requires to control and reduce these heats. However, these heat components are originated from very different mechanisms, some of which can be controlled through advanced OET strategies, but some are inherently related to the intrinsic properties of the materials or devices and cannot be modified very conveniently. Therefore, a comprehensive OET analysis is strongly needed in order to distinguish these heat processes for further optimizations.
1) For the thermalization-induced heat, it is only determined by the material bandgap so that $P_{\mathrm{th}}$ is a constant value irrespective to the applied bias [see Figure 3(c)]. A popular method to minimize this heat generation is to build the gradient bandgap material or double-/multi-junction configuration.16, 4446 2) Increasing the forward bias, the built-in electric field in the PSC is weakened, which degrades the carrier-separation capability. Therefore, the recombination current shows a monotonic increment and leads to the gradually increased recombination heat. Practically, the optimization of carrier transportation dynamics (e.g., preparing the high-quality film and engineering the energy level alignment) can significantly dwindle the recombination heat. $^{16-25}\ 3$ ) For the Joule heat, it is peaked at $V=0$ , i.e., with the strongest built-in electric field; however, the Joule heat is decreased sharply under a high enough forward bias since the current density and the built-in electric field are both significantly decreased. Unfortunately, the Joule heat is usually regarded as the self-heating effect in SC and can hardly be modified.10 4) The Peltier heats $P_{P e l t i e r}^{S S}$ and $P_{P e l t i e r}^{S M})$ are only related to the current density for a specific PSC; thus, they change along with the current density. Despite so, the Peltier heats can still be effectively depressed by reducing the energy band offset between heterojunction and semiconductor-metal interfaces (see Equations 78).
Under these thermodynamic effects, the typical working temperature of a SC with one-sun illumination could reach $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ or higher.11 The raised temperature destructively degrades the output power and operation stability of the device.1215 This is particularly important for PSCs since the temperature stability is a critical consideration for this kind of SC. Here we estimate the operating temperature and quantitatively explore the temperature rise of PSC under the effects of the abovementioned heat mechanisms. As shown in Figure S4(a) (Supporting information), our result demonstrates that the practical operating temperature of the discussed PSC reaches $49.79^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (background temperature: $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) at the operation point of SC. For more detailed information, we list in Table 1 the temperature rises due to various heat mechanisms. As can be seen that the thermalization is indeed the dominant energy loss in PSC, which could lift the device temperature by about $\sim10.99\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Peltier, Joule, and recombination heats take also the principal portions in heating the device, leading to the overall temperature increment of ${\sim}11.42\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The parasitic heat absorptions by FTO, HTL and Au take $\sim2.38$ $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
After the quantitative analysis of the energy losses in PSCs, a reasonable question now is “Is it possible to modulate the carrier thermodynamics for device optimizations?” Actually, the modulation and optimization of the PSC operation can be achieved by controlling many parameters. For example, the doping concentrations can regulate the carrier transportation by regulating the built-in electric field, the depletion width, and the ionized free-carrier concentration.8,30 Figures S5(a) and S5(b) (Supporting information) display the $J_{-}V$ characteristics of the PSC under various perovskite and HTL doping concentrations, respectively. With increasing the perovskite doping, the Fermi-level splitting and builtin electric field become stronger, leading to higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $F F$ ; however, the separation of the photogenerated carriers becomes less efficient due to the narrowed depletion region and thus leads to a stronger carrier recombinations and a lower $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . However, increasing the doping concentration of HTL does not qualitatively affect $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , but significantly improves the $F F$ .30
The above observations on the device performance parameters can be further discussed through analyzing the power consumptions by Joule heat, total carrier recombination, and Peltier heat $(i.e.,P_{\mathrm{Joule}},$ $P_{\mathrm{U}},P_{\mathrm{Peltier}})$ as shown in Figures 4(a) and 4(b), respectively. It should be noted that the Joule heat shows a sharp decrement with increasing the perovskite doping concentration due to the narrowed depletion region. $P_{P e l t i e r}^{S S}$ is almost unchanged since the current density does not noticeably change. On the other hand, if we increase the HTL doping concentration, the energy-level difference between the hole quasiFermi level and valence band will be decreased, leading to a lower $P_{P e l t i e r}^{S M}$ . However, a higher doping concentration of HTL implies the stronger force of built-in electric field, causing the higher $P_{\mathrm{{Joule}}}$ . The bulk recombination is also slightly decreased along this process. Therefore, an overall effect is that the power consumption by the heat-generation mechanisms is reduced and thus leads to the higher performance as shown in Figure S5(b) (Supporting information). As well, it should be noted that tuning the doping concentration of ETL, the similar results can be observed.
In addition, literatures have demonstrated that the effectively matched energy levels of the heterojunction PSCs can enable a better optoelectronic performance;24,25 however, we find that it only works when the quality of the perovskite film is poor, i.e., with very short carrier lifetime (e.g., 10 ns200 ns). If a longer carrier lifetime $(e.g.,\sim2~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ is considered in the simulation, the energy level alignment shows a very weak effect on the $J_{-}V$ characteristics [Figures S5(c) and S5(d), Supporting information]. However, the energy alignments do show manifest influences on the carrier thermodynamic characteristics [Figures 4(c) and 4(d)]. As can be seen, with increasing the energy-level difference of heterojunction ( $\Delta E_{\mathrm{c}}$ and $\Delta E_{\mathrm{v}}^{\mathrm{\Delta\cdot}}$ ), the $P_{P e l t i e r}^{S S}$ is almost lineally increased and the $P_{\mathrm{U}}$ nearly unchanged since the current density does not noticeably change. However, the $P_{\mathrm{{Joule}}}$ is lineally decreased along with the raise of energy level difference since a large energy level difference implies a larger built-in potential difference. Particularly, it should be noticed that the sum of $P_{P e l t i e r}^{S S}$ and $P_{\mathrm{{Joule}}}$ , which can be defined as the intrinsic losses in heterojunction PSC, is almost a constant irrespective to tuning the energy level alignment between perovskite/HTL or ETL. The related intrinsic heat at the maximum power point (MPP) can be defined as:
$$
\ H_{\mathrm{intrinsic}}~=~\left(\mathrm{E_{g}-V_{M P P}}\right)\cdot\J_{M P P}
$$
where $H_{\mathrm{{intrinsic}}}$ is the overall intrinsic heat including the Joule and Peltier heats, $V_{\mathrm{MPP}}$ and $J_{{M P P}}$ are the forward bias and current density at MPP, respectively.
Such an OET study allows quantitatively addressing the various energy losses in PSCs as well as providing optimization roadmap for PSCs for higher performances. Our results are displayed in Figure 5(a), where the PCE of $57.87\%$ is an ideal situation by spectrally integrating the solar spectrum within the whole absorption band of the perovskite material. The PCE of the realized PSC in our experiment is $21.37\%$ , which is obviously far below the ideal value, due to a number of losses, including optical reflection (leading to PCE loss of ${\sim}8\%$ ), thermalization (PCE loss: $13.2\%$ ), Joule and Peltier (PCE loss: $12\%$ ), carrier recombination (PCE loss: $1.7\%$ ), and parasitic resistance (PCE loss: $1.6\%$ . Here, the optical loss can be readily controlled by using the advanced photonic designs. The losses by the carrier recombination and parasitic resistance losses are relatively low, but can still be further reduced by carrier transportation optimization (via semiconductor junction design) and device fabrication optimization. Importantly, we can see that the thermalization, Joule and Peltier losses are particularly strong, which greatly limit the performance achieved. To reduce the thermalization loss, the straightforward strategy is to introduce tandem or multi-junction setup (we can also use up/downconverting or other methods). However, the Joule and Peltier losses belong to the intrinsic type and can hardly be effectively controlled. Therefore, we can see that there are indeed several kinds of energy loss channels in PSCs and have to be suppressed in order to further improve the performance of PSCs.
As an example, here we show that the OET simulation can be utilized to design the high-performance PSCs. From optical perspective, we focus on simultaneously reducing the reflection and parasitic absorption of FTO layer. Therefore, we design the light-trapping structure of FTO gating with the use of a thin FTO layer, as inserted in Figure 5(b). The absorption of PSC features the significant enhancement, leading to higher external quantum efficiency (EQE) in a broad band $(400-790\mathrm{nm}$ ), as shown in Figure S6 (Supporting information). In order to minimize the electrical losses, a thinner perovskite layer (e.g., $400\mathrm{nm}$ ) together with a higher doping concentration $(1{\times}10^{17}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ are considered to obtain a lower bulk recombination in PSC. Thermally, the reduced parasitic absorption and lower recombination leads to a lower operating temperature. Figure 5(b) shows that the PCE of the planar PSC can be enhanced from
$21.37\%$ to $23.84\%$ after our OET optimization. It should note that if the intrinsic material properties (e.g., carrier lifetime and mobility) are further optimized, even higher PCEs could be possible.
Finally, we would like to indicate that the proposed OET simulation technology can be readily applied to other semiconductor-based SCs by properly modifying the mathematical model and the device structure, together with employing the corresponding material parameters (e.g., bandgap, affinity, density of states, carrier lifetime and mobility, etc.).34,35,47 This is because most of the semiconductorbased SCs are working under the similar semiconductor principles, i.e., with various PN junctions composed by different kinds of materials. Indeed, these SCs have also the similar heat generation and passivation channels, e.g., thermalization, recombination, Joule and Peltier heats, discussed in this study. Even for organic SCs, by properly modifying the model to incorporate the special carrier separation and recombination mechanisms in organic systems, a similar study can be realized as well. Therefore, the OET simulation can find a very broad application for the device-level multiphysics study of different kinds of SCs.
## Conclusions
In summary, we present a comprehensive opto-electro-thermal study of the heterojunction perovskite solar cells in order to realize a deep insight into the complicated multiphysics behaviors and guide the optimal design of this kind of solar cell. The complete OET simulation is implemented by addressing the electromagnetic response, the detailed electron/hole dynamics, as well as the intrinsic thermodynamic processes in inherently coupled optical, electrical, and thermal domains.
The detailed OET model and the thermodynamic processes in the heterojunction PSC are first presented. A planar PSC is then fabricated and tested experimentally, which shows a PCE of $21.02\%$ . We realize an agreement between the device simulation and the experimental measurement. Benefited from the simulation, the microscopic carrier thermodynamic behaviors and energy loss channels arising from the coupled OET factors are scrutinized. Six categories of microscopic heat conversion processes in the heterojunction PSC are discussed. We find that the sum of Joule and Peltier heats is a constant value at maximum power point, which can be defined as the intrinsic loss of PSC. We further demonstrate that the operation temperature of PSC under AM 1.5 illumination can reach about $49.79\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; however, the planar PSC has a better temperature property than Si-based SCs, since it has much less performance degradation with the increase of the temperature. We further present an OET-coupled optimal design for the PSC, which shows an increased PCE from $21.37\%$ to $23.84\%$ according to the OET prediction. Finally, a design roadmap by OET strategies is proposed for PSCs.
The heterojunction OET study can be used to explore the underlying microscopic physics and further guide the design of high-performance photovoltaic devices; moreover, the proposed OET model can be extended to investigate the fundamental sciences of various optoelectronic and thermoelectric devices, including light-emitting diodes (LEDs), photodetectors, lasers, or thermoelectric coolers.
## Method/Experiment
Materials. Lead iodide $\mathrm{(PbI_{2}}$ , Alfa Aesar, $99.9985\%$ ), MAI (Advanced Election Technology Co., Ltd), FAI (Xi’an polymer), lead thiocyanate $(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ (Sigma-Aldrich, $99.5\%)$ ), dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, Sigma-Aldrich, $99.8\%$ , and N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, Sigma-Aldrich, $99.8\%$ ) were purchased and used directly. We first made $\mathrm{MA_{0.7}F A_{0.3}P b I_{3}}$ precursor by dissolving mixed FAI, MAI, $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{SCN}})_{2}$ powders in mixed DMF and (DMSO solvents. The solutions were stirred overnight on a $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ hot plate before deposition. The amount of $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{SCN})_{2}$ in perovskite precursors is $2.0\%$ with respect to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ .
Fabrication of Solar Cells. Our PSC has the regular cell configuration: Glass/FTO/ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{60}.$ $\mathrm{SAM/MA_{0.7}F A_{0.3}P b I_{3}}$ /Spiro-OMeTAD/Au. Here, FTO is fluorine-doped tin oxide ( $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ :F), $\mathrm{C}_{60}$ -SAM is self-assembled fullerene monolayer, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is the electron selective layer (ESL) deposited by spincoating the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ aqueous solution Spiro-OMeTAD is 2,2’,7,7’-tetrakis(N,N’-di-pmethoxyphenylamine)-9,9’-spirobifluorene hole selective layer (HSL), co-doped with lithium and cobalt salts. The perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated on the ESL first at $500\mathrm{rpm}$ for 3s, and then at 4000 rpm for 60s with diethyl ether as the anti-solvent agent dropped on the film at $10~\mathrm{s}$ of second step. The as prepared perovskite film was annealed on a hot plate at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 2 minutes and then $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 minutes. The typical thickness of our perovskite layer is about $620\mathrm{nm}$ . The Spiro-OMeTAD solution was prepared as we have reported previously.37 All of the fabrication processes were conducted in a ${\bf N}_{2}$ filled glove box.
Characterizations. $J_{-}V$ characteristics were tested in the laboratory under standard test conditions (STCs), that is, the working temperature of PSC at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $A M\ 1.5\mathrm{G}$ with power density of 100 $\mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . A solar simulator (Enli Tech) was used as the light source to measure the $J_{-}V$ curve, and a
Keithley 2400 sourcemeter was used to record the data. The $J_{-}V$ curve in Figure 2 was performed under reverse voltage scan from $1.2{\mathrm{~V~}}$ to $-0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ with a sweeping speed of $1\ \mathrm{V/s}$ . Field emission SEM instrument (Hitachi S-4800) was used to characterize the top view and cross-sectional structures of the perovskite films and PSCs. For steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) measurements, perovskite films were prepared by spin-coating perovskite precursors on a soda lime glass (SLG) substrates and then coated with another polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) layer. The transmission and absorbance spectrum of the perovskite layer was measured using the Lambda 1050s ultraviolet-visible-near infrared (UV-vis-NIR) spectrometer.
